# Git Staging & Commits

Do **not** run `git add`, `git commit`, or otherwise stage/commit changes unless the user **explicitly** asks in the current message (e.g. “commit this”, “create a commit”, “git add and commit”).

## Hard rules

- Completing a task, checklist, or “Done When” item is **not** permission to commit — even if the task doc says to commit.
- Do not infer commit permission from earlier messages; require an explicit ask **now**.
- Do not stage files “in preparation” for a later commit.
- After finishing work, leave changes unstaged/uncommitted and tell the user what changed; they can ask to commit when ready.

## Allowed without an explicit commit ask

- Read-only git commands: `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git branch`, etc.
- Code/file edits the user requested.

## When the user does ask to commit

Follow the project’s committing protocol (status/diff/log, draft message, stage only relevant files, commit via HEREDOC, never push unless also asked).
